/-!
# Universal quotient flattening ideal over a henselian local ring

Source: Stacks Project, flat.tex, Tag 05PG (`theorem-flattening-local`), restricted
to local quotient maps, including its final finite-generation assertion
(blueprint: lem-sfb-henselian-fp-module-quotient-flattening-ideal).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w z

namespace Algebra

/-- A finitely presented module over a local essentially finitely presented algebra of a
henselian local ring has a finitely generated ideal detecting flatness of all local quotients.
The presentation witness is `C`, with multiplicative subset `S`; the scalar towers specify
that the maps and module actions are compatible. The quotient module has Mathlib's canonical
`A / J`-action, with `JP = J • (⊤ : Submodule A P)`.

Source: Stacks Project, flat.tex, Tag 05PG (`theorem-flattening-local`), quotient-base
specialization (blueprint: lem-sfb-henselian-fp-module-quotient-flattening-ideal).

Proof:
1. Finite presentation implies finite type for the algebra and finite generation for the
   module. Use `lem-sfb-henselian-pointed-devissage-chart` to obtain a pointed complete
   devissage after a faithfully flat local extension. Faithful flatness after base change
   (`lem-weil-pbc-faithfully-flat-base-change`) and
   `lem-trf-flatness-faithfully-flat-extension` detect quotient flatness on this chart.
   The maps `x ⊗ a ↦ ax mod J` and `x mod J ↦ x ⊗ 1` identify tensor and quotient modules,
   by `lem-weil-pbc-tensor-right-exact`. The first surjection and
   `lem-trf-unique-prime-localization` identify its localized module with the first stage;
   the same identifications apply to successive cokernels and commute with quotienting.
2. Induct backwards on the finite devissage. Construct an ideal at each stage detecting
   localized quotient flatness, while retaining that the whole stage module modulo that
   ideal is projective. The terminal zero cokernel has ideal zero and is projective.
   For an empty chain the chart module, hence the original module, is zero; take ideal zero.
3. Write `K` for the ideal of the next cokernel. If quotienting the current localized module
   by `J` makes it flat, `lem-trf-local-generic-basis-universal-injective` after base change
   makes the basis map modulo `J` universally injective and its localized cokernel flat.
   Thus `K ≤ J`. The hypotheses survive the quotient: flatness by
   `lem-weil-pbc-flat-module-base-change`, finite presentation coefficientwise, geometric
   integrality on the same fibres, and the marked generic fibre since `J ≤ m`. This also
   follows from `lem-sfb-complete-algebra-devissage-base-change`.
4. Over `R = A/K` the global cokernel is projective by induction. Split the exact sequence
   with image `E` using `lem-weil-pbc-ses-projective-quotient-splits`. The free finite-rank
   module `F` over the stage algebra is projective over `R` by
   `lem-trf-local-fibres-integral-projective`. Choose a split embedding `j : F → G` into a
   free `R`-module. Let `L` be generated by all coordinates of `j(x)` for `x` in the kernel
   of the surjection `F → E`. No finite generation of this kernel is asserted.
5. For every `R`-algebra `H`, tensor right exactness identifies the kernel of `F_H → E_H`
   with the image of the tensor of that kernel. The split embedding remains injective,
   and the tensor of `G` is free on the same basis, by `e_t ⊗ h ↦ he_t` and its inverse.
   Hence this surjection is an isomorphism exactly when all the generating coordinates
   vanish in `H`, equivalently `L` maps to zero. The split cokernel sequence shows this
   is also exactly injectivity of the basis map over `H`, including nonreduced `H`.
6. Pull `L` back to an ideal `I_i` of `A`. Apply step 3 at `J = m`: every module over the
   residue field is flat (`lem-weil-pbc-module-over-field-flat`), so step 5 gives `I_i ≤ m`.
   For any quotient making the localized module flat, steps 3 and 5 give `I_i ≤ J`.
   Conversely modulo `I_i` the split sequence identifies the whole module with the direct
   sum of a finite power of the stage algebra and the cokernel. Both are projective by
   step 4, induction, and `lem-trf-local-projective-tensor-base-change`; their direct sum
   is projective by `lem-weil-pbc-projective-direct-sum`. Further scalar extension keeps
   it projective, hence flat by `lem-weil-pbc-projective-module-flat`. Localization preserves
   base flatness by `lem-pc-affine-flat-module-localization-preserves-flatness`.
7. The first stage ideal `I` now has the desired criterion, by step 1. Its finitely generated
   subideals form a nonempty directed family with union `I`: use zero, sums, and principal
   subideals respectively. Since `P/IP` is flat, `lem-sfb-fp-quotient-flatness-directed-limit`
   gives one finitely generated subideal `I₀` with flat quotient. The criterion implies
   `I ≤ I₀`, whence equality and finite generation. This uses finite presentation over `B`,
   and does not require either ring to be Noetherian.
-/
theorem exists_henselian_fp_module_quotient_flattening_ideal
    (A : Type u) (B : Type v) (C : Type w)
    [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [CommRing C]
    [HenselianLocalRing A] [IsLocalRing B]
    [Algebra A B] [IsLocalHom (algebraMap A B)]
    [Algebra A C] [Algebra C B] [IsScalarTower A C B]
    [Algebra.FinitePresentation A C] (S : Submonoid C) [IsLocalization S B]
    (P : Type z) [AddCommGroup P] [Module B P] [Module A P] [IsScalarTower A B P]
    [Module.FinitePresentation B P] :
    ∃ I : Ideal A, I.FG ∧ I ≤ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A ∧
      ∀ J : Ideal A, J ≤ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A →
        (Module.Flat (A ⧸ J) (P ⧸ J • (⊤ : Submodule A P)) ↔ I ≤ J) := by
  sorry

end Algebra
